Confirmed. The issue is exactly what I suspected:
- For `𝛼` (U+1D6FC, an astral character in `ID_Continue`):
  - `char[0]` is the high surrogate `\uD835`
  - `ID_CONTINUE.test(char[0])` returns `false` (because the surrogate is not in `ID_Continue`)
  - But `ID_CONTINUE.test(char)` returns `true` (the full character IS in `ID_Continue`)
- For `😀` (U+1F600, an astral character NOT in `ID_Continue`):
  - `ID_CONTINUE.test(emoji)` returns `false` (correct)
  - `ID_START.test(emoji)` is `false` (also correct)

So the fix is to use `codePointAt(0)` or use array spread to get the full character. The cleanest fix is to use the spread operator or `codePointAt(0)`.

Let me now look at the relevant code in `src/index.ts`:

```js
function stringifyName(name: string, next: Token | undefined): string {
  if (!ID.test(name)) return quoteName(name);

  if (next?.type === "text" && ID_CONTINUE.test(next.value[0])) {
    return quoteName(name);
  }

  return name;
}
```

The fix is to use `String.prototype.codePointAt(0)` to get the full code point, or to use `[...next.value][0]` to get the first full character.

The simplest and most efficient fix is to use `codePointAt(0)` since it avoids array allocation:

```js
